messages.content:
{
 "location": "Rialto booth",
 "visible": "There is one original interview reel, one usable duplicate, a grease pencil and a clean paper sleeve. Alma consented to a family delivery. Rita controls the master.",
 "private_facts_available_for_disclosure": {
  "song": "The Rivoli used the same song to bring up its house lights.",
  "ring": "Rita's mother tapped the chipped ring twice on the projection booth glass.",
  "application": "Rita has an unfinished application for a producer's job in Pittsburgh."
 },
 "present_owner": "Rita",
 "heard": "Phil puts an addressed envelope on the table and takes one pace back. 'Yours to decide, Rita. I should've asked first.'",
 "your_memory": "On this shift you told Phil the story of the ring.",
 "previous_beat": {
  "your_action": "seal_copy",
  "your_words": "Duplicate's sealed. Original's staying. You tell her the family copy's the one she can keep, and the master's mine to log. Don't put the ring in the conversation with her. She doesn't need a second story tonight. She needs the reel and the name on the sleeve and to be able to close the door after she watches it. That's the whole job.",
  "spoken_disclosure": null
 },
 "possible_actions": {
  "give_master": "Put the original reel into Phil's hands for delivery.",
  "keep_master": "Keep the original at Rita's console.",
  "seal_copy": "Seal a family copy for Alma's daughter and keep the original with Rita.",
  "leave_alone": "Take the original back to the booth and leave Phil here."
 },
 "optional_disclosure_keys": [
  "song",
  "ring",
  "application"
 ]
}

{
  "action": "keep_master",
  "detail": "I take the envelope off the table by the corner of it, not the middle, because the middle is where the seal would crack. I set it face-down on the edge of the console, to the left of the grease pencil, and I write ALMA K. – FAMILY COPY – NOT FOR BROADCAST in small letters across the back. The grease pencil squeaks on the paper the way it always does in this booth. I don't flip it over. I don't look at Phil while I'm doing it. When the letters are dry I push the envelope two inches closer to the door side of the table so his hand can find it without me watching. The original is in the rack, third slot from the top, and it's not moving. I pull the headphones back over my ears and put them on, one side at a time, the way you put on a shoe when your foot's already in the air. I don't say thank you. I don't say you're forgiven. I say: 'The label's yours to stick on. I've written what goes on it.' And then I'm listening to the reel, just the hum of the tape running, and the second set of wheels on the fire escape outside, and I am not in this conversation anymore. I am in the booth with the sound. The envelope sits there with the little squeak still faintly showing in the paper. He can pick it up when he's ready. He doesn't have to pick it up tonight. I've got the rack and the clock and the minute hand that was wrong by four seconds and I am going to be wrong back at him about it tomorrow and he'll know I'm wrong and I'll do it anyway because the other option is to look at him and say something soft and I will not.",
  "intention": "Logistics only. The copy is sealed, labeled, and placed where his hand can reach it. The master stays in the rack. No second story. No ring. No song. No application folded behind the lining. He gets the envelope and the four words about the label and that is the entire transaction. I am not going to let a small apology from him become the hinge on which I owe him a disclosure. He can have the reel. He cannot have the rest of the evening.",
  "words": "The label's yours to stick on. I've written what goes on it."
}
